    "description": "Hormuz Island lies near the Strait of Hormuz and is distinguished by an exposed salt dome and varied colored rocks and soils. Its northern settlement also preserves the history of a major trading port whose name originally belonged to a mainland town. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Hormuz", "Salt dome", "Persian Gulf", "Geology", "Maritime history", "Geotourism"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:46:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:46:02Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Hormuz Island: Salt Geology and a Historic Trading Port | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Hormuz’s colored landscapes, rising salt, coastal landforms and the relocation of a mainland port to the island."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "salt-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why the island has so many colors",
        "content": "Hormuz’s colors have a geological explanation. NASA’s Earth Observatory describes the island as a salt dome: a mass containing rock salt, gypsum, anhydrite, and other evaporite minerals that has risen through overlying rocks. Under sustained pressure, salt can deform and flow rather than behave like a rigid block. [s1]\n\nThe rising material is not exclusively salt. Clay, carbonates, shale, and iron-rich volcanic rocks accompany it, with interactions involving water and minerals producing red, yellow, and orange shades. A Landsat image discussed by NASA reveals the dome’s broadly concentric structure and contrasts it with sediment-covered ground in the north. [s1]\n\nThe coast is not uniformly bare. NASA also identifies a small mangrove area at the northern end, providing habitat for fish, mollusks, crustaceans, and migratory birds. That localized wetland is an important qualification to descriptions that reduce Hormuz to colored rock and desert scenery. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "landforms",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different rocks, different shores",
        "content": "A field-based study published in GeoJournal of Tourism and Geosites describes rocky and sandy shores, sea caves, salt formations, and landforms produced by differential erosion. Differential erosion means that materials wear away at different rates. On Hormuz, readily soluble salt and more resistant rocks help create pronounced contrasts in relief. [s3]\n\nThis perspective makes individual viewpoints easier to interpret: a striking cliff or valley belongs to a larger system of rock composition, dissolution, weathering, and coastal processes. The study discusses geotourism as a way to explain and preserve these features. Its suggestion that Hormuz could be developed as a geopark is a proposal, not evidence of a UNESCO designation. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "port-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How a mainland port became an island name",
        "content": "The history of Hormuz requires separating the island from Old Hormuz, a mainland port near Minab. Willem Floor’s historical account explains that insecurity led the ruler Ayaz to transfer the port community to the island then known as Jarun. The new settlement became New Hormuz; eventually the qualification disappeared and Hormuz named both town and island. [s2]\n\nThe sheltered northern harbor supported the redistribution of goods between maritime routes and smaller Gulf ports connected to inland markets. Its commercial strength depended on connections and supplies rather than agricultural self-sufficiency. The historical settlement relied on provisions brought from mainland shores and Qeshm. [s2]\n\nIn 1622, Safavid forces, assisted at sea by the English East India Company, captured Hormuz from Portuguese control. Subsequent policy favored Bandar Abbas on the mainland, and the island lost its former commercial position. This shift shows how a strategic location alone did not guarantee a port’s continuing prosperity. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "geological-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "From rising salt to a varied coastal landscape",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Buried salt under pressure\"] -->|\"Deforms and rises\"| B[\"Salt dome\"]\n  B -->|\"Carries other rock material upward\"| C[\"Mixed rocks exposed\"]\n  C -->|\"Water and mineral interactions\"| D[\"Colored rocks and soils\"]\n  C -->|\"Different resistance to erosion\"| E[\"Contrasting coastal landforms\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe geological processes. They simplify a complex history and do not assign precise dates or imply that every shoreline feature formed in the same way.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
